br();hd('ABSTRACT')
para('CyberGuard is a web-based firewall rule configuration and testing lab intended to make packet-filter behavior easier to demonstrate and inspect. It combines a browser dashboard, Python Flask application, persistent SQLite storage, a local packet simulation engine and an iptables command backend. It supports rule creation, filtering, deletion, activity history, policy status, JSON export and packet-verdict explanations. The implementation emphasizes a safe development posture: the backend defaults to lab mode and dry run, constructs command argument arrays, validates rule fields, binds the web server to loopback and includes an anti-lockout check with a timed rollback path for Linux live application.')
para('This report documents the need, related engineering practices, requirements, architecture, design alternatives, implementation and validation evidence visible in the supplied repository. The simulator evaluates rules in database order and returns the first matching action or the selected chain policy. SQLite stores rules, logs and settings. The interface uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript and communicates with Flask through JSON requests. The backend supports a limited IPv4 filter-rule subset; it does not claim IPv6 or production firewall-management coverage.')
para('The project contains an automated unit-test module for backend validation, dry-run behavior, lockout protection, command ordering and counter parsing. The repository does not contain evidence that the real Linux virtual-machine test plan has been executed. The supplied Windows checkout cannot demonstrate live kernel enforcement, and its local virtual environment lacks Flask, so a live API session is not claimed. Figures in this report are labeled as source-derived illustrations; code extracts are copied from project files.')
para('The result is an educational prototype for explaining rule order, default policies and safeguards around direct firewall changes. It is not a production firewall product. Further work should strengthen authentication, rule semantics, atomic application, IPv6 coverage, audit integrity, automated integration testing and deployment controls.')
br();hd('GRAPHICAL ABSTRACT');pic(fig['arch'],6.0);cap('Figure GA.1: Components and enforcement boundary');para('The browser presents rule and simulation workflows. Flask validates and coordinates requests. SQLite stores project state. The backend translates validated objects into iptables argument lists. In the reviewed Windows checkout, command execution remains disabled by the backend live-mode conditions.')
