ch(1,'Introduction')
chapter1=[
('1.1 Background and project context',['Network communication requires decisions about which traffic a host accepts, rejects or forwards. Linux Netfilter provides packet filtering in the kernel, and iptables is a user-space interface for managing rule tables and chains. The same flexibility creates operational risk: an incorrect default policy or poorly ordered rule can interrupt a legitimate service or remote management session.','A terminal-only exercise can teach syntax while leaving rule order and packet outcomes difficult to interpret. CyberGuard combines a dashboard, a safe packet simulator and a guarded command backend. The system is intended as an educational demonstration, not as a replacement for production firewall management.']),
('1.2 User need and stakeholders',['The primary users are students studying computer networks and cybersecurity. Instructors and evaluators need a repeatable demonstration that does not require root access on an ordinary development machine. A learner needs to create rules, inspect their fields, test hypothetical packets, and understand whether an outcome comes from a specific rule or a chain policy.','The repository establishes an educational lab context but does not name an external client, institution or production operator. This report therefore treats students and instructors as its intended users. The title page and certificate retain blank fields for personal and institutional information not present in the supplied files.']),
('1.3 Contemporary engineering issue',['Firewall configuration crosses the boundary between application logic and high-impact system state. A dashboard can make changes easier, so its execution boundary must be explicit. CyberGuard routes browser requests through Flask, validates structured data, and delegates command building to a backend that can return non-executed previews.','The project also presents a usability-security tradeoff. A shared token is simple for classroom demonstration but does not provide per-user identity or strong accountability. The README recommends loopback binding, SSH tunneling for VM access, a restricted sudo rule and an unprivileged Flask process.']),
('1.4 Problem identification',['The problem is the gap between firewall syntax and understandable policy behavior. Learners need to define simple IPv4 rules, predict packet outcomes and recognize lockout risk without accidentally changing a daily-use host. The application also needs persistent state so a demonstration survives reloads.','The project does not attempt to solve every firewall administration problem. Its purpose is to connect structured rule data to an explanation, persistent storage and a carefully gated path to kernel commands. The Linux VM validation plan is documented, but completed VM results were not present in the repository.']),
('1.5 Scope and exclusions',['The supported rules cover INPUT, OUTPUT and FORWARD; TCP, UDP, ICMP and ALL; IPv4 address/CIDR values; optional ports; and ACCEPT, DROP or REJECT actions. The application provides rule CRUD, search and filtering, simulation, logs, export, status and health endpoints backed by SQLite.','The backend is limited to basic IPv4 filter-chain operations. IPv6, NAT, custom chains, interface matching, stateful rule semantics, multi-user authorization, distributed operation and production deployment are outside the supplied implementation. Other firewall managers can conflict, and synchronization may remove rules manually added to managed chains.']),
('1.6 Goals, objectives and deliverables',['The goal is a safe and understandable firewall lab. Objectives are to visualize policy and rule state, persist rules and activity, simulate first-match evaluation, reject malformed values, default to non-executing command mode, and include a basic anti-lockout and recovery path. Deliverables are the Flask application, backend, HTML template, JavaScript, CSS, SQLite database, requirements file, unit-test definitions, README and this report.','Success for the educational objective means that the learner can distinguish a rule match from a policy fallback and can verify that safe mode is active. Success for real enforcement requires separate Linux VM evidence: connectivity checks, command outcomes, counter observations and rollback validation.']),
('1.7 Timeline and report organization',['The README describes three project phases: interactive frontend and simulation, Flask/SQLite integration, then guarded iptables integration. The repository has no dated sprint journal, so this timeline reflects project phase organization rather than specific historical dates. A sound plan moves from requirements to simulation, persistence, guarded integration, VM validation and reporting.','Chapter 2 reviews firewall behavior and implementation sources. Chapter 3 describes requirements, alternatives, architecture, constraints and design decisions. Chapter 4 records source-based results and identifies evidence still needed. Chapter 5 concludes and proposes future work. Appendices provide a user manual, API and schema references, validation scenarios, source listings and project management material.'])]
for t,ps in chapter1:block(t,ps)
