ch(2,'Literature Survey')
chapter2=[
('2.1 Review method and bibliometric limits',['This review uses primary technical documentation and standards relevant to firewall policy, Netfilter, subprocess invocation, Flask and SQLite. It is an engineering survey, not a systematic review of scholarly databases. The project files do not include search exports or publication counts, so a quantitative bibliometric analysis cannot be reproduced honestly.','Instead, the review maps each source theme to a design implication. A future academic extension can define databases, search strings, date ranges, inclusion criteria and a citation dataset before reporting counts or trends.']),
('2.2 Firewall purpose and policy',['NIST Special Publication 800-41 Rev. 1 discusses firewall technologies and recommendations for policy, configuration, testing, deployment and management [4]. The guidance supports treating a firewall as an operational lifecycle, not only as a command string. CyberGuard provides a small educational subset of that lifecycle.','RFC 2979 describes firewall behavior and interoperability considerations, including avoidance of unintended failures for legitimate protocol usage [1]. A simple packet simulator helps explain a decision but does not model path MTU discovery, connection state or application-level behavior.']),
('2.3 Netfilter chains and rule order',['iptables manages tables, built-in chains, rules and default policies [3]. A rule combines match conditions with a target. These concepts map to the project fields for chain, protocol, addresses, ports and action. The visible ordering of rules matters because an earlier matching rule determines the result before later entries are considered.','CyberGuard sorts stored rules by identifier and returns the first match. That behavior is deliberately understandable but does not establish complete parity with kernel traversal. Baseline rules, chain flushes and command ordering affect the effective live ruleset, so parity requires direct VM observation.']),
('2.4 Validation and safe process invocation',['The frontend checks common address and port syntax to improve usability. Server-side checks remain authoritative because a client can be bypassed. The Flask application normalizes user-facing field names, while the backend validates and converts addresses and ports before constructing commands.','Python subprocess documentation supports executing programs with argument sequences, timeouts and controlled output [6]. The backend uses an argument list and shell=False, adding a boundary against shell parsing. Input validation and restricted executable permissions remain necessary even when shell parsing is disabled.']),
('2.5 Persistence, web APIs and auditability',['Flask provides route and request abstractions and supports JSON responses, matching the project API model [5]. SQLite provides local relational tables and default values [7]. CyberGuard stores rules, settings and logs in a single database file; this is appropriate to a modest local demonstration but does not include migrations or enterprise operational management.','Mutation routes use a shared API token, while several GET endpoints are open to callers who can reach the local listener. The browser receives the token through the rendered page, so this is not individual authentication. Logs are useful local activity history but lack tamper-evident protection and can be cleared.']),
('2.6 Operational safety and project position',['A restrictive INPUT policy can interrupt remote administration. The backend checks for a TCP SSH accept rule, inserts loopback and established-connection baseline entries, backs up the ruleset in live mode and schedules rollback unless confirmed. These controls reduce selected risks but do not cover every management path or make multiple commands atomic.','The project contribution is pedagogical integration: a local simulator and a guarded backend share a browser workflow. The main engineering themes are explicit policy, predictable order, validation, cautious operating-system integration and lifecycle testing. Production authentication, complete protocol behavior and live VM evidence remain open needs.']),
('2.7 Problem definition and objectives',['Students need a safe tool to define simple IPv4 firewall rules, reason about packet outcomes and understand risks of restrictive policies while preserving demonstration data. Existing command-line practice can be powerful but difficult to interpret and potentially hazardous if used on a live host.','The project goal is a local web lab that demonstrates structured rule management, first-match simulation, activity history and an explicitly gated Linux enforcement path. Its objectives are policy visualization, persistent records, validation, safe defaults and reproducible VM testing.'])]
for t,ps in chapter2:block(t,ps)
tab('Table 2.1: Literature themes and engineering implications',['Theme/source','Implication','Project relationship'],[('NIST SP 800-41 Rev. 1','Policy includes selection, configuration and testing','Dashboard plus VM plan'),('RFC 2979','Filtering should avoid gratuitous protocol failures','Simulator limitations stated'),('Netfilter documentation','Chains, rules, targets and policies form command model','Built-in chain subset'),('Python subprocess docs','Use controlled argument sequences and timeouts','shell=False backend'),('Flask documentation','Route and JSON request handling','Flask API'),('SQLite documentation','Schema and constraints shape persistence','Three local tables')])
ch(3,'Design Flow and Process')
chapter3=[
('3.1 Concept generation and selection',['Three concepts were considered: a command-line rule worksheet, a browser-only simulator, and a layered web application with Flask, SQLite and an optional guarded backend. The first is straightforward to test but less visual; the second is safe and easy to present but lacks persistence and server boundaries.','The layered design is selected because it connects a learner action to a database record, a deterministic simulation and a command preview. It has higher setup complexity, so safe defaults and explicit status reporting are important. Selection reflects educational value and does not imply production readiness.']),
('3.2 Functional and non-functional requirements',['Functional requirements include showing firewall state, managing rules, filtering records, simulating packets, explaining verdicts, storing logs, exporting JSON, reporting backend health and guarding live synchronization. The route declarations and UI elements provide source evidence for these requirements.','Non-functional requirements include safety, usability, portability, maintainability, testability, reliability and observability. They are design goals rather than measured service-level guarantees. The implementation is locally oriented and the Linux enforcement path depends on the platform, binaries and external sudo configuration.']),
('3.3 System architecture',['The browser loads the template and static assets. JavaScript retrieves status, rules and logs through Flask JSON endpoints. Flask handlers validate data and interact with SQLite. The simulator evaluates packets against stored rules. The backend translates validated rule objects into argument arrays and gates actual execution according to platform and environment flags.','There are two distinct decision paths: local simulation and optional kernel enforcement. They share rule concepts but are not proven equivalent. Safe mode makes the simulation path usable without privileged operations, while Linux VM parity tests remain necessary before interpreting command behavior as verified.']),
('3.4 Rule lifecycle and packet algorithm',['Rule creation begins in a modal with chain, protocol, addresses, ports and action. A live command preview updates as fields change. Client validation identifies common errors, while Flask and backend validation protect the server boundary. The database stores the accepted object and returns its identifier.','Packet simulation checks active rules in stable ID order. A candidate must satisfy chain, protocol and applicable address/port constraints. The first matching action ends evaluation. If no rule matches, the chain policy decides. The returned trace names the matching rule or fallback. The model excludes connection tracking, interfaces, NAT, custom chains and IPv6.']),
('3.5 Apply lifecycle, recovery and data model',['For synchronization, the backend validates rules and policies, checks the SSH lockout condition, then performs a sequence that temporarily opens policies, flushes built-in chains, inserts baseline entries, adds stored rules and applies final policies. In live mode, it saves a backup and schedules rollback unless confirmation arrives within the configured interval.','This is not an atomic transaction. Partial command failures and timer/process behavior need VM testing. The data model contains rules, logs and key/value settings. Initial seed rows provide demonstration data and counters; they are not telemetry. Schema creation is embedded in app.py and lacks versioned migration support.']),
('3.6 Constraints and social responsibility',['Technical constraints include IPv4-only rules, Linux-only live execution, required iptables utilities, a restricted sudo configuration and a single local SQLite file. Operational constraints include remote-access preservation, conflict risk with Docker or other firewall managers and the need to keep a recovery console available.','Security and ethical practice require transparent labeling of simulated data, limited collection of real logs, protection of secrets and authorization before monitoring traffic. The checked-in default token is a development placeholder. Screenshots should use documentation/example addresses and should not expose personal or production information.']),
('3.7 Economic, environmental and management considerations',['The system uses open-source software and does not require specialized hardware. Its principal non-software cost is development and a disposable Linux VM for live validation. A snapshot supports repeatable experiments and reduces recovery cost. Modest compute requirements make the prototype suitable for classroom machines.','A staged plan establishes requirements, builds the interface and simulator, adds API and persistence, introduces guarded integration, validates in a VM, then prepares the report. Checkpoints should preserve outputs and record environment details. The README supports communication, while API responses, command previews and logs help explain behavior.'])]
for t,ps in chapter3:block(t,ps)
