ch(4,'Results Analysis and Validation')
chapter4=[
('4.1 Implementation environment and evidence boundary',['The supplied project contains Flask source, the iptables backend, HTML template and standalone page, JavaScript, CSS, SQLite data, requirements and a unittest module. The checked-out environment is Windows and the project virtual environment lacks Flask. Therefore a local API session and live dashboard capture were unavailable during preparation of this report.','Source inspection supports statements about route definitions, schema, validation and safety gates. It cannot prove that a browser session, Linux kernel change, live counter update or rollback occurred. The report marks the dashboard visual as an illustrative reconstruction and treats the documented VM plan as planned validation.']),
('4.2 Frontend and API implementation',['The template defines Dashboard, Firewall Rules, Packet Tester, Activity Log and Lab Topology sections. JavaScript loads status, rules and logs, renders metrics, builds table rows, validates the rule form, creates the command preview, sends JSON requests and updates activity output. The CSS defines a light visual theme and interface layout.','The Flask app exposes rule listing, creation, deletion, simulation, firewall status/toggle/confirm, live counters, log listing/clearing/export and health. A token decorator protects selected mutation routes. Several read endpoints are not protected, and the token is rendered to the browser; therefore the mechanism is not an identity system.']),
('4.3 Persistence and guarded backend',['SQLite initialization creates rules, logs and settings tables, seeds default policy settings and inserts example rules when the rules table is empty. Seeded packet and byte counters are demonstration values. The database helper uses row objects and explicit connection closing. SQL writes use parameters in inspected paths.','The backend checks for Linux platform and required binaries as well as disabled safe-mode flags before setting live true. It builds command argument lists and uses shell=False with timeout. It validates IPv4/CIDR, ports, protocol, chain and action; ports are restricted to TCP/UDP. Anti-lockout and timed rollback are implemented in source.']),
('4.4 Validation strategy and test inventory',['The supplied unittest module defines cases for valid command generation, injection-like inputs, invalid addresses and ports, unsupported chains/actions, invalid protocol/port combinations, dry-run non-execution, lockout refusal, policy order and counter parsing. Their presence demonstrates intended coverage but does not mean they were executed as part of this report.','System validation should distinguish unit, API integration and kernel VM levels. The README lists health, SSH acceptance with INPUT DROP, confirmation, lockout refusal, rollback, deletion, counters and malformed-input checks. No completed VM result log was located.']),
('4.5 Interpretation, limitations and results',['Source evidence shows a coherent educational sequence from user input through Flask and SQLite to simulation, with a separate guarded backend path. It supports explanation of rule fields, default policies and operational controls. It does not establish performance, kernel correctness, production security, interoperability or deployment reliability.','Known limitations include the shared default token, incomplete endpoint protection, IPv4-only rules, single-process database assumptions, potential conflict with other firewall managers, non-atomic multi-command updates, and differences between simulator and kernel semantics. The backend rebuilds built-in chains, so manually inserted rules can be removed during synchronization.']),
('4.6 Tools and communication',['The project combines Python, Flask, SQLite, HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Linux iptables and Python unittest. Human-readable verdicts, rule IDs, status badges, command previews and activity logs support demonstration. Report figures and matrices expose architecture and validation status, while actual runtime evidence should be captured separately during the VM milestone.','The current report does not claim measured throughput, packet latency or completed user-study results. Future performance work should specify rule count, request rate, test host and repeat count. A small application can still benefit from reproducible evidence and precise reporting.'])]
