tab('Table 4.3: Limitations and action',['Limitation','Effect','Recommended action'],[('Default token','Unsafe if exposed','Replace and add identities'),('Public GET routes','Local disclosure','Define endpoint permissions'),('IPv4 only','No IPv6 protection','Add IPv6 or nftables support'),('Non-atomic sync','Partial state possible','Staged apply and recovery'),('Example counters','May look like observations','Label seed data'),('No VM results','Cannot claim live success','Execute test plan and record evidence')])

ch(5,'Conclusion and Future Work')
chapter5=[
('5.1 Conclusion',['CyberGuard combines a browser dashboard, Flask API, SQLite persistence, deterministic packet simulation and an optional iptables backend for teaching firewall policy. It exposes rule fields and chain policies, simulates first-match outcomes, retains demonstration records and includes safeguards around command construction and live changes.','The strongest documented contribution is educational integration rather than a new filtering algorithm. Safe-mode defaults, shell-free argument invocation, validation, a basic SSH lockout check, baseline rules and a rollback timer are useful prototype controls. The code also defines tests for selected backend properties.']),
('5.2 Deviation from expected results',['The README lays out a Linux VM plan for kernel verification, counters, deletion and rollback. The supplied files do not contain evidence of completed VM tests. Live behavior must therefore be described as implemented but unverified.','The local Windows virtual environment lacks Flask, preventing a running session in this report workflow. The interface image is labeled illustrative, and code extracts are source-derived rather than screenshots of a live IDE. These distinctions prevent an illustration from being mistaken for observed execution.']),
('5.3 Future work',['Strengthen authentication and authorization by replacing the shared token, assigning roles, protecting read endpoints, securing transport and adding CSRF defenses. Keep secrets outside source control and make configuration validation explicit.','Improve recovery by serializing apply operations, checking backup integrity, reporting restore failures, handling repeated applies and testing process restart. Consider a staged or transactional design so a command failure does not leave a partially changed ruleset.','Align simulator semantics with the intended kernel subset. Formalize the rule schema, add property-based tests, test protocol-specific combinations and compare predictions against a disposable Linux VM. Add IPv6 only after data model, UI and parity requirements are clear.','Add API integration tests, database migration support, sanitized VM evidence, repeatable performance measurement, structured logs and API versioning. Expand NAT, custom chains or interface matching only with clearly scoped requirements and test coverage.']),
('5.4 Way ahead',['The immediate next milestone is controlled Linux VM validation, not public deployment. Begin from a snapshot, preserve console access, use a restricted sudo allow-list and a strong local token, keep loopback binding and connect through an SSH tunnel. Execute the documented checks and retain outputs.','After testing, resolve simulator/kernel mismatches and add repeatable integration tests. Broader deployment should wait for stronger authentication, endpoint authorization, audit integrity, atomic recovery and review of firewall-manager interactions. Until then the product should remain labeled as a learning lab.'])]
for t,ps in chapter5:block(t,ps)
