br();hd('APPENDIX 1  USER MANUAL')
para('This manual describes the local safe-mode workflow represented by the project. The commands below are instructions from the project README, not actions performed as part of this report. Use an isolated Linux VM for live kernel changes.')
manual=[
('A1.1 Prerequisites',['Use Python 3.9 or newer as stated in the README. Install Flask and Flask-Cors in an isolated environment. Keep the project directory intact so template and static paths resolve. For live work, use an Ubuntu Server VM, install iptables and take a snapshot before making changes.','Keep console access available and use a second VM if network traffic is required. The Windows checkout is not a live iptables target. Confirm the active interpreter and dependency set before starting the service.']),
('A1.2 Safe startup',['Open a terminal in the `firewall-lab` folder, install packages from requirements.txt and start with `python app.py`. Visit `http://127.0.0.1:5000`. The README sets LAB_MODE and DRY_RUN to true by default. Check the health route and status badge before demonstrating a preview.','The server binds to loopback. Keep this bind address during a local presentation. If the page fails, inspect startup output for missing packages and confirm port 5000 is available. Do not broaden the listener to troubleshoot.']),
('A1.3 Dashboard navigation',['The left navigation opens Dashboard, Firewall Rules, Packet Tester, Activity Log and Lab Topology. The dashboard presents active state, counts, default policies and recent rules. Values come from the API or the sample database and are not proof of kernel state.','Use Refresh Rules to reload data. A lab-mode badge indicates configuration, not necessarily real enforcement. The initial database can insert example rules and illustrative packet counts when it is empty.']),
('A1.4 Add a rule',['Open Add Rule and choose INPUT, OUTPUT or FORWARD; choose TCP, UDP, ICMP or ALL; enter IPv4/CIDR values; add optional ports only for TCP or UDP; and select ACCEPT, DROP or REJECT. Preset chips provide example values.','Review the generated command preview. It is not executed by the browser. Submit, then inspect the notification and table. Correct any fields the server rejects. Use only documentation/example addresses in demonstrations and report figures.']),
('A1.5 Search and deletion',['Use text search, chain and action filters to narrow the table. The play control prefills the packet tester. The delete control opens a confirmation dialog; verify the ID and rule fields before confirming.','In dry-run mode, deletion of a database record does not prove that a kernel rule was removed. Kernel consistency belongs to the Linux VM test sequence.']),
('A1.6 Packet simulation',['Open Packet Tester and enter a source, protocol, destination port and chain. Choose a packet expected to match a known rule, run simulation, and inspect the verdict, explanation and matched-rule card. Then test a packet that matches no explicit rule to demonstrate the chain policy fallback.','The simulator does not send packets. Its model is intentionally narrower than the kernel and omits connection state, interfaces, NAT, custom chains and IPv6. Explain the rule ordering as part of the demonstration.']),
('A1.7 Activity log and export',['Open Activity Log to review tags, messages, results and timestamps. Export JSON downloads the records. Treat this as local operational history, not tamper-proof evidence. Seed records are examples.','Clearing logs deletes database rows. Do this only when demonstration records are disposable. Remove sensitive details from exports and screenshots before sharing.']),
('A1.8 Live mode in a Linux VM',['Follow the README to identify exact executable paths and configure the restricted sudoers allow-list. Set a strong token and explicitly disable both safe-mode flags only on the VM. Run Flask unprivileged, preserve loopback binding and access via SSH tunnel.','Before applying INPUT DROP, add the correct TCP SSH ACCEPT rule. Review the rules, ensure console recovery and watch the confirmation deadline. Confirm only after checking connectivity. If not confirmed, the backend intends to restore the saved ruleset.']),
('A1.9 Troubleshooting and shutdown',['If API data does not load, inspect Flask output and browser console, verify token configuration and confirm database write permission. If live mode is false, inspect platform, binaries and flags. A successful dry-run response means only that the command was prepared.','Stop the process after demonstration. Check for pending rollback state and inspect final rules from the VM console. Revert the snapshot if the exercise leaves unexpected policy.'])]
for t,ps in manual:block(t,ps)
